        "selector": "/objectives/0/text"
      },
      "claim": "The objective says a browser blocks a cross-origin response. That is overbroad: cross-origin script requests are restricted, some requests are sent without a preflight, and the server can process them; without the required CORS opt-in, the response is not shared with the requesting script. This distinction matters for both debugging and CSRF-safe server design.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CORS",
          "section": "Cross-Origin Resource Sharing overview; Functional overview; Simple requests",
          "retrievedAt": "2026-10-12T21:21:21Z",
          "textSha256": "461b2248bc85e86b2b90637b3f1fd9327f243c78fba70d3ac6c4da37c2aa6e0a"
        }
      ],
      "recommendation": "Reword o1 to explain that the browser restricts frontend JavaScript from reading a cross-origin response unless CORS permits it, and that only requests outside the CORS-safelisted conditions trigger a preflight. Preserve the fixed CORS topic and its Spring/Vite comparison.",
      "reviewedSha256": "a09cc856eedbf0f944e05bc4d0249f470ae1eb3fe4274eb108c5c697042047ca"
